 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Shark NZ801UK anti hair wrap upright - suction has gone really weak over the last couple of weeks, barely picks up crumbs. Have emptied the bin. Any ideas before I buy a new one?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Switch it off and unplug it (or take the battery off) first. You don't need to buy a new one yet; weak suction is usually fixable. The next most likely cause is clogged filters. Please check if they were washed and fully dry for 24 hours (damp filters choke airflow). Also, detach the hose and wand and look through them to ensure there are no blockages inside.\n[Helpful to see: image \"Poor suction — what to check first\" — Lost suction is usually a full bin, a dirty filter or a blockage in the hose, tube or floor head — check and clear those before suspecting the motor. Switch off and unplug first. Typical layout — your model may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Filters have never been washed, didn't know they were there. Hose and wand are clear, coin went through the hose and I can see light through the wand."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Since the hose and wand are clear, the most likely cause of gradual suction loss on this model is clogged filters. Shark vacuums have two filters that trap fine dust; when they get blocked, airflow drops significantly even if the bin is empty. Please locate them (usually behind a flap near the bin or at the top), wash them in cold water only, and leave them to dry for 24 hours—damp filters will choke suction completely. While you are there, turn the floorhead over and check if hair is wrapped tightly around the brush bar, as this also restricts airflow."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Washed both filters in lukewarm water and left them 24 hours. Massive difference, suction is like new. Brushroll just had a bit of fluff and spins freely. No burning smell at any point either."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That is excellent news. The filters were most likely the culprit; Shark vacuums rely on them for airflow, and once they get clogged with fine dust, suction drops significantly even if the bin looks empty. To keep it running well, try to wash those filters every few months and always let them dry completely (24 hours) before putting them back in. If you need anything else, just ask."
  }
 ],
